From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jake Cattrall <krazyjakee@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 10:39:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Size an auto-height column flex container from its items'
 hypothetical main sizes

A column flex container whose height is auto took its intrinsic main
size from its items' content, through the flex fraction algorithm. Chrome
instead sums the items' hypothetical main sizes: each item's
flex base size clamped by its max-height and floored by its min-height (an
explicit one, or the automatic minimum, which brings the content back when
min-height is auto).

The two disagree for an item with `flex: 1 1 0` and an explicit
min-height whose content is taller: Chrome sizes the column from the
min-height and keeps the overflow inside the item, Taffy grew the column
to the full content. A `min-height: 100%` column holding a growing row
with an `overflow: scroll` child is a common way to fill a view with a
scroll area; under Taffy the scroll area grew to its whole content (55,000
px instead of the view's 600) and a virtualised list inside it mounted
every row.

Two Chrome-generated fixtures cover it: the explicit-min case (fails
before this change) and the automatic-minimum case (passes either way).
